  "prompt_text": "The figure is a map of Africa highlighting the North African countries and summarizing basic health and socioeconomic indicators for each. Algeria, Tunisia, Libya, Egypt, Morocco, Sudan, and Western Sahara are shaded in purple and linked to callout boxes listing poverty rate, population, life expectancy, and mortality.\n\nTaken together, the map emphasizes regional variation across North Africa. Tunisia and Morocco show the lowest displayed poverty levels, while Sudan and Libya have much higher poverty, with Sudan standing out as the worst case overall. Egypt has by far the largest population, whereas Western Sahara has a very small population and some missing data. Life expectancy is generally in the low- to mid-70s for most of the region, but it appears notably lower in Sudan and Western Sahara. Mortality also varies substantially: Tunisia and Libya have relatively low values, while Sudan is much higher than the others.\n\nThe overall message is a comparison of public health and demographic conditions across North Africa, showing that countries in the same geographic region can differ markedly in poverty burden, survival, and mortality. The rest of Africa is shown in muted gray mainly for geographic context.",
